DRUG: Acyclovir suspension (3efa1e5e-5a14-4724-a2ca-25702f10faed)
  PASSAGE_0000: 'DESCRIPTION\nAcyclovir, USP is a synthetic nucleoside analogue active against herpes viruses. Acyclovir Oral Suspension, USP is a formulation for oral administration. Each teaspoonful (5 mL) of acyclovir oral suspension, USP contains 200 mg of acyclovir, USP and the inactive ingredients carboxy methyl cellulose sodium, glycerin, methylparaben, microcrystalline cellulose, propylparaben, purified water, sorbitol solution and banana flavor (propylene glycol, Nat & Art flavor). Acyclovir, USP is a white or almost white crystalline powder with the molecular formula C8H11N5O3 and a molecular weight of 225.20.\xa0 Acyclovir, USP is soluble in water, freely soluble in alkaline water, very slightly soluble in ethanol and most organic solvents. The pka’s of acyclovir are 2.16 and 9.04. The chemical name of acyclovir, USP is 2-amino-1,9-dihydro-9-((2-hydroxyethoxy)methyl)-6H-purin-6-one; it has the following structural formula:\nVIROLOGY Mechanism of Antiviral Action\nAcyclovir is a synthetic purine nucleoside analogue with in vitro and in vivo inhibitory activity against herpes simplex virus types 1 (HSV-1), 2 (HSV-2), and varicella-zoster virus (VZV). The inhibitory activity of acyclovir is highly selective due to its affinity for the enzyme thymidine kinase (TK) encoded by HSV and VZV. This viral enzyme converts acyclovir into acyclovir monophosphate, a nucleotide analogue. The monophosphate is further converted into diphosphate by cellular guanylate kinase and into triphosphate by a number of cellular enzymes. In vitro, acyclovir triphosphate stops replication of herpes viral DNA. This is accomplished in 3 ways: 1) competitive inhibition of viral DNA polymerase, 2) incorporation into and termination of the growing viral DNA chain, and 3) inactivation of the viral DNA polymerase. The greater antiviral activity of acyclovir against HSV compared to VZV is due to its more efficient phosphorylation by the viral TK.\nAntiviral Activities\nThe quantitative relationship between the in vitro susceptibility of herpes viruses to antivirals and the clinical response to therapy has not been established in humans, and virus sensitivity testing has not been standardized. Sensitivity testing results, expressed as the concentration of drug required to inhibit by 50% the growth of virus in cell culture (IC50), vary greatly depending upon a number of factors. Using plaque-reduction assays, the IC50 against herpes simplex virus isolates ranges from 0.02 to 13.5 mcg/mL for HSV-1 and from 0.01 to 9.9 mcg/mL for HSV-2. The IC50 for acyclovir against most laboratory strains and clinical isolates of VZV ranges from 0.12 to 10.8 mcg/mL. Acyclovir also demonstrates activity against the Oka vaccine strain of VZV with a mean IC50 of 1.35 mcg/mL.\nDrug Resistance\nResistance of HSV and VZV to acyclovir can result from qualitative and quantitative changes in the viral TK and/or DNA polymerase. Clinical isolates of HSV and VZV with reduced susceptibility to acyclovir have been recovered from immunocompromised patients, especially with advanced HIV infection. While most of the acyclovir-resistant mutants isolated thus far from immunocompromised patients have been found to be TK-deficient mutants, other mutants involving\xa0 the viral TK gene (TK partial and TK altered) and DNA polymerase have been isolated. TK-negative mutants may cause severe disease in infants and immunocompromised adults. The possibility of viral resistance to acyclovir should be considered in patients who show poor clinical response during therapy.'
  PASSAGE_0001: 'Pharmacokinetics\nThe\xa0pharmacokinetics\xa0of\xa0acyclovir\xa0after\xa0oral\xa0administration\xa0have\xa0been\xa0evaluated in\xa0healthy\xa0volunteers\xa0and\xa0in\xa0immunocompromised\xa0patients\xa0with\xa0herpes\xa0simplex\xa0or varicella-zoster virus infection. Acyclovir pharmacokinetic parameters are summarized in Table 1.'
  PASSAGE_0002: 'Table\xa01.\xa0Acyclovir\xa0Pharmacokinetic\xa0Characteristics\xa0(Range)\n*Bioavailability\xa0decreases\xa0with\xa0increasing\xa0dose.\nIn\xa0one\xa0multiple-dose,\xa0crossover\xa0study\xa0in\xa0healthy\xa0subjects\xa0(n\xa0=\xa023),\xa0it\xa0was\xa0shown\xa0that\xa0increases in\xa0plasma\xa0acyclovir\xa0concentrations\xa0were\xa0less\xa0than\xa0dose\xa0proportional\xa0with\xa0increasing\xa0dose,\xa0as\xa0shown in Table 2.\xa0The decrease in bioavailability is a function of the dose and not the dosage form.\nTable\xa02.\xa0Acyclovir\xa0Peak\xa0and\xa0Trough\xa0Concentrations\xa0at\xa0Steady\xa0State\nThere\xa0was\xa0no\xa0effect\xa0of\xa0food\xa0on\xa0the\xa0absorption\xa0of\xa0acyclovir\xa0(n\xa0=\xa06);\xa0therefore,\xa0acyclovir oral suspension may\xa0be\xa0administered\xa0with\xa0or\xa0without\xa0food.\nThe\xa0only\xa0known\xa0urinary\xa0metabolite\xa0is\xa09-(carboxymethoxy)methyl]guanine.'
  PASSAGE_0003: 'Adults With\xa0Impaired\xa0Renal\xa0Function\nThe\xa0half-life\xa0and\xa0total\xa0body clearance\xa0of\xa0acyclovir\xa0are\xa0dependent\xa0on\xa0renal\xa0function.\xa0A\xa0dosage\xa0adjustment\xa0is\xa0recommended\xa0for patients\xa0with\xa0reduced\xa0renal\xa0function\xa0.'
  PASSAGE_0004: 'Geriatrics\nAcyclovir\xa0plasma\xa0concentrations\xa0are\xa0higher\xa0in\xa0geriatric\xa0patients\xa0compared\xa0with younger\xa0adults,\xa0in\xa0part\xa0due\xa0to\xa0age-related\xa0changes\xa0in\xa0renal\xa0function.\xa0Dosage\xa0reduction\xa0may\xa0be\xa0required in\xa0geriatric patients with underlying renal impairment .'
  PASSAGE_0005: 'Pediatrics\nIn\xa0general,\xa0the\xa0pharmacokinetics\xa0of\xa0acyclovir\xa0in\xa0pediatric\xa0patients\xa0is\xa0similar\xa0to\xa0that of\xa0adults.\xa0Mean\xa0half-life\xa0after\xa0oral\xa0doses\xa0of\xa0300\xa0mg/m2\xa0and\xa0600\xa0mg/m2\xa0in\xa0pediatric\xa0patients\xa0aged 7\xa0months\xa0to\xa07\xa0years\xa0was\xa02.6\xa0hours\xa0(range\xa01.59\xa0to\xa03.74\xa0hours).'
  PASSAGE_0006: 'Drug\xa0Interactions\nCoadministration\xa0of\xa0probenecid\xa0with\xa0intravenous\xa0acyclovir\xa0has\xa0been\xa0shown\xa0to increase\xa0the\xa0mean\xa0acyclovir\xa0half-life\xa0and\xa0the\xa0area\xa0under\xa0the\xa0concentration-time\xa0curve.\xa0Urinary excretion\xa0and\xa0renal\xa0clearance\xa0were\xa0correspondingly\xa0reduced.'
  PASSAGE_0007: 'Initial\xa0Genital\xa0Herpes\nDouble-blind,\xa0placebo-controlled\xa0studies\xa0have demonstrated that orally administered acyclovir significantly reduced the duration of acute infection and duration of lesion healing. The duration of pain and new lesion formation was decreased in some patient groups.'
  PASSAGE_0008: 'Recurrent\xa0Genital\xa0Herpes\nDouble-blind, placebo-controlled studies in patients with frequent recurrences (6 or more episodes per year) have shown that orally administered acyclovir given daily for 4 months to 10 years prevented or reduced the frequency and/or severity of recurrences in greater than 95% of patients. In a study of patients who received acyclovir 400 mg twice daily for 3 years, 45%, 52%, and 63% of patients remained free of recurrences in the first, second, and third years, respectively. Serial analyses of the 3-month recurrence rates for the patients showed that 71% to 87% were recurrence free in each quarter.'
  PASSAGE_0009: 'Herpes\xa0Zoster\xa0Infections\nIn a double-blind, placebo-controlled study of immunocompetent patients with localized cutaneous zoster infection, acyclovir (800 mg 5 times daily for 10 days) shortened the times to lesion scabbing, healing, and complete cessation of pain, and reduced the duration of viral shedding and the duration of new lesion formation. In a similar double-blind, placebo-controlled study, acyclovir (800 mg 5 times daily for 7 days) shortened the times to complete lesion scabbing, healing, and cessation of pain; reduced the duration of new lesion formation; and reduced the prevalence of localized zoster-associated neurologic symptoms (paresthesia, dysesthesia, or hyperesthesia). Treatment was begun within 72 hours of rash onset and was most effective if started within the first 48 hours. Adults greater than 50 years of age showed greater benefit.'
  PASSAGE_0010: 'Chickenpox\nThree randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials were conducted in 993 pediatric patients aged 2 to 18 years with chickenpox. All patients were treated within 24 hours after the onset of rash. In 2 trials, acyclovir was administered at 20 mg/kg 4 times daily (up to 3,200 mg per day) for 5 days. In the third trial, doses of 10, 15, or 20 mg/kg were administered 4 times daily for 5 to 7 days. Treatment with acyclovir shortened the time to 50% healing; reduced the maximum number of lesions; reduced the median number of vesicles; decreased the median number of residual lesions on day 28; and decreased the proportion of patients with fever, anorexia, and lethargy by day 2. Treatment with acyclovir did not affect varicella-zoster virus-specific humoral or cellular immune responses at 1 month or 1 year following treatment.'
  PASSAGE_0011: 'Herpes\xa0Zoster\xa0Infections\nAcyclovir\xa0is\xa0indicated\xa0for\xa0the\xa0acute\xa0treatment\xa0of\xa0herpes\xa0zoster (shingles).'
  PASSAGE_0012: 'Genital\xa0Herpes\nAcyclovir\xa0is\xa0indicated\xa0for\xa0the\xa0treatment\xa0of\xa0initial\xa0episodes\xa0and\xa0the\xa0management\xa0of recurrent\xa0episodes\xa0of\xa0genital\xa0herpes.'
  PASSAGE_0013: 'Chickenpox\nAcyclovir\xa0is\xa0indicated\xa0for\xa0the\xa0treatment\xa0of\xa0chickenpox\xa0(varicella).'
  PASSAGE_0014: 'Acyclovir\xa0is\xa0contraindicated\xa0for\xa0patients\xa0who\xa0develop\xa0hypersensitivity\xa0to\xa0acyclovir\xa0or valacyclovir.'
  PASSAGE_0015: 'Acyclovir Oral Suspension is intended for oral ingestion only. Renal failure, in some cases resulting in death, has been observed with acyclovir therapy . Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura/hemolytic uremic syndrome (TTP/HUS), which has resulted in death, has occurred in immunocompromised patients receiving acyclovir therapy.'
  PASSAGE_0016: 'Dosage\xa0adjustment\xa0is\xa0recommended\xa0when\xa0administering\xa0acyclovir\xa0to\xa0patients\xa0with\xa0renal impairment\xa0.\xa0Caution\xa0should\xa0also\xa0be\xa0exercised\xa0when administering\xa0acyclovir\xa0to\xa0patients\xa0receiving\xa0potentially\xa0nephrotoxic\xa0agents\xa0since\xa0this\xa0may\xa0increase the\xa0risk\xa0of\xa0renal\xa0dysfunction\xa0and/or\xa0the\xa0risk\xa0of\xa0reversible\xa0central\xa0nervous\xa0system\xa0symptoms\xa0such\xa0as those\xa0that\xa0have\xa0been\xa0reported\xa0in\xa0patients\xa0treated\xa0with\xa0intravenous\xa0acyclovir.\xa0Adequate\xa0hydration\xa0should be\xa0maintained.'
  PASSAGE_0017: 'Patients are instructed to consult with their physician if they experience severe or troublesome adverse reactions, they become pregnant or intend to become pregnant, they intend to breastfeed while taking orally administered acyclovir, or they have any other questions.\nPatients\xa0should\xa0be\xa0advised\xa0to\xa0maintain\xa0adequate\xa0hydration.'
  PASSAGE_0018: 'Herpes\xa0Zoster\nThere are no data on treatment initiated more than 72 hours after onset of the zoster rash. Patients should be advised to initiate treatment as soon as possible after a diagnosis of herpes zoster.'
  PASSAGE_0019: 'Genital\xa0Herpes\xa0Infections\nPatients should be informed that acyclovir is not a cure for genital herpes. There are no data evaluating whether acyclovir will prevent transmission of infection to others. Because genital herpes is a sexually transmitted disease, patients should avoid contact with lesions or intercourse when lesions and/or symptoms are present to avoid infecting partners. Genital herpes can also be transmitted in the absence of symptoms through asymptomatic viral shedding. If medical management of a genital herpes recurrence is indicated, patients should be advised to initiate therapy at the first sign or symptom of an episode.'
  PASSAGE_0020: 'Chickenpox\nChickenpox in otherwise healthy children is usually a self-limited disease of mild to moderate severity. Adolescents and adults tend to have more severe disease. Treatment was initiated within 24 hours of the typical chickenpox rash in the controlled studies, and there is no information regarding the effects of treatment begun later in the disease course.'
  PASSAGE_0021: 'See CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY:\xa0Pharmacokinetics.'
  PASSAGE_0022: 'The data presented below include references to peak steady-state plasma acyclovir concentrations observed in humans treated with 800 mg given orally 5 times a day (dosing appropriate for treatment of herpes zoster) or 200 mg given orally 5 times a day (dosing appropriate for treatment of genital herpes). Plasma drug concentrations in animal studies are expressed as multiples of human exposure to acyclovir at the higher and lower dosing schedules .\nAcyclovir was tested in lifetime bioassays in rats and mice at single daily doses of up to 450 mg/kg administered by gavage. There was no statistically significant difference in the incidence of tumors between treated and control animals, nor did acyclovir shorten the latency of tumors. Maximum plasma concentrations were 3 to 6 times human levels in the mouse bioassay and 1 to 2 times human levels in the rat bioassay.\nAcyclovir was tested in 16 in vitro and in vivo genetic toxicity assays. Acyclovir was positive in 5 of the assays.\nAcyclovir did not impair fertility or reproduction in mice (450 mg/kg/day, p.o.) or in rats (25 mg/kg/day, s.c.). In the mouse study, plasma levels were 9 to 18 times human levels, while in the rat study, they were 8 to 15 times human levels. At higher doses (50 mg/kg/day, s.c.) in rats and rabbits (11 to 22 and 16 to 31 times human levels, respectively) implantation efficacy, but not litter size, was decreased. In a rat peri- and post-natal study at 50 mg/kg/day, s.c., there was a statistically significant decrease in group mean numbers of corpora lutea, total implantation sites, and live fetuses.\nNo testicular abnormalities were seen in dogs given 50 mg/kg/day, IV for 1 month (21 to 41 times human levels) or in dogs given 60 mg/kg/day orally for 1 year (6 to 12 times human levels).\nTesticular atrophy and aspermatogenesis were observed in rats and dogs at higher dose levels.'
  PASSAGE_0023: 'Acyclovir administered during organogenesis was not teratogenic in the mouse (450 mg/kg/day, p.o.), rabbit (50 mg/kg/day, s.c. and IV), or rat (50 mg/kg/day, s.c.). These exposures resulted in plasma levels 9 and 18, 16 and 106, and 11 and 22 times, respectively, human levels.\nThere are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. A prospective epidemiologic registry of acyclovir use during pregnancy was established in 1984 and completed in April 1999. There were 749 pregnancies followed in women exposed to systemic acyclovir during the first trimester of pregnancy resulting in 756 outcomes. The occurrence rate of birth defects approximates that found in the general population. However, the small size of the registry is insufficient to evaluate the risk for less common defects or to permit reliable or definitive conclusions regarding the safety of acyclovir in pregnant women and their developing fetuses. Acyclovir should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.'
  PASSAGE_0024: 'Acyclovir concentrations have been documented in breast milk in 2 women following oral administration of acyclovir and ranged from 0.6 to 4.1 times corresponding plasma levels. These concentrations would potentially expose the nursing infant to a dose of acyclovir up to 0.3 mg/kg/day. Acyclovir should be administered to a nursing mother with caution and only when indicated.'
  PASSAGE_0025: 'Safety and effectiveness of oral formulations of acyclovir in pediatric patients younger than 2 years of age have not been established.'
  PASSAGE_0026: 'Of 376 subjects who received acyclovir in a clinical study of herpes zoster treatment in immunocompetent subjects ≥50 years of age, 244 were 65 and over while 111 were 75 and over. No overall differences in effectiveness for time to cessation of new lesion formation or time to healing\xa0 were reported between geriatric subjects and younger adult subjects. The duration of pain after healing was longer in patients 65 and over. Nausea, vomiting, and dizziness were reported more frequently in elderly subjects. Elderly patients are more likely to have reduced renal function and require dose reduction. Elderly patients are also more likely to have renal or CNS adverse events. With respect to CNS adverse events observed during clinical practice, somnolence, hallucinations, confusion, and coma were reported more frequently in elderly patients .'
  PASSAGE_0027: 'Short-Term\xa0Administration\nThe most frequent adverse events reported during clinical trials of treatment of genital herpes with acyclovir 200 mg administered orally 5 times daily every 4 hours for 10 days were nausea and/or vomiting in 8 of 298 patient treatments (2.7%). Nausea and/or vomiting occurred in 2 of 287 (0.7%) patients who received placebo.'
  PASSAGE_0028: 'Long-Term\xa0Administration\nThe most frequent adverse events reported in a clinical trial for the prevention of recurrences with continuous administration of 400 mg (two 200-mg capsules) 2 times daily for 1 year in 586 patients treated with acyclovir were nausea (4.8%) and diarrhea (2.4%). The 589 control patients receiving intermittent treatment of recurrences with acyclovir for 1 year reported diarrhea (2.7%), nausea (2.4%), and headache (2.2%).'
  PASSAGE_0029: 'Herpes\xa0Zoster\nThe most frequent adverse event reported during 3 clinical trials of treatment of herpes zoster (shingles) with 800 mg of oral acyclovir 5 times daily for 7 to 10 days in 323 patients was malaise (11.5%). The 323 placebo recipients reported malaise (11.1%).'
  PASSAGE_0030: 'Chickenpox\nThe most frequent adverse event reported during 3 clinical trials of treatment of chickenpox with oral acyclovir at doses of 10 to 20 mg/kg 4 times daily for 5 to 7 days or 800 mg 4 times daily for 5 days in 495 patients was diarrhea (3.2%). The 498 patients receiving placebo reported diarrhea (2.2%).'
  PASSAGE_0031: 'Observed\xa0During\xa0Clinical\xa0Practice\nIn addition to adverse events reported from clinical trials, the following events have been identified during post-approval use of acyclovir. Because they are reported voluntarily from a population of unknown size, estimates of frequency cannot be made. These events have been chosen for inclusion due to either their seriousness, frequency of reporting, potential causal connection to acyclovir, or a combination of these factors.\nGeneral:\nAnaphylaxis, angioedema, fever, headache, pain, peripheral edema.\nNervous:\nAggressive\xa0behavior,\xa0agitation,\xa0ataxia,\xa0coma,\xa0confusion,\xa0decreased\xa0consciousness, delirium,\xa0dizziness,\xa0dysarthria,\xa0encephalopathy,\xa0hallucinations,\xa0paresthesia,\xa0psychosis,\xa0seizure, somnolence,\xa0tremors.\xa0These\xa0symptoms\xa0may\xa0be\xa0marked,\xa0particularly\xa0in\xa0older\xa0adults\xa0or\xa0in\xa0patients\xa0with renal\xa0impairment\xa0.\nDigestive:\nDiarrhea, gastrointestinal distress, nausea.\nHematologic\xa0and\xa0Lymphatic:\nAnemia, leukocytoclastic vasculitis, leukopenia, lymphadenopathy, thrombocytopenia.\nHepatobiliary\xa0Tract\xa0and\xa0Pancreas:\nElevated liver function tests, hepatitis, hyperbilirubinemia, jaundice.\nMusculoskeletal:\nMyalgia.\nSkin:\nAlopecia, erythema multiforme, photosensitive rash, pruritus, rash, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, urticaria.\nSpecial\xa0Senses:\nVisual\xa0abnormalities.\nUrogenital:\nRenal\xa0failure, renal pain (may be associated with renal failure), elevated\xa0blood\xa0urea\xa0nitrogen,\xa0elevated\xa0creatinine,\xa0hematuria\xa0.'
  PASSAGE_0032: 'Overdoses\xa0involving\xa0ingestion\xa0of\xa0up\xa0to\xa0100\xa0capsules\xa0(20\xa0g)\xa0have\xa0been\xa0reported.\xa0Adverse\xa0events that\xa0have\xa0been\xa0reported\xa0in\xa0association\xa0with\xa0overdosage\xa0include\xa0agitation,\xa0coma,\xa0seizures,\xa0and\xa0lethargy. Precipitation\xa0of\xa0acyclovir\xa0in\xa0renal\xa0tubules\xa0may\xa0occur\xa0when\xa0the\xa0solubility\xa0(2.5\xa0mg/mL)\xa0is\xa0exceeded\xa0in the\xa0intratubular\xa0fluid.\xa0Overdosage\xa0has\xa0been\xa0reported\xa0following\xa0bolus\xa0injections\xa0or\xa0inappropriately\xa0high doses\xa0and\xa0in\xa0patients\xa0whose\xa0fluid\xa0and\xa0electrolyte\xa0balance\xa0were\xa0not\xa0properly\xa0monitored.\xa0This\xa0has resulted\xa0in\xa0elevated\xa0BUN\xa0and\xa0serum\xa0creatinine\xa0and\xa0subsequent\xa0renal\xa0failure.\xa0In\xa0the\xa0event\xa0of\xa0acute\xa0renal failure\xa0and\xa0anuria,\xa0the\xa0patient\xa0may\xa0benefit\xa0from\xa0hemodialysis\xa0until\xa0renal\xa0function\xa0is\xa0restored\xa0.'
  PASSAGE_0033: 'Acute\xa0Treatment\xa0of\xa0Herpes\xa0Zoster\n800\xa0mg\xa0every\xa04\xa0hours\xa0orally,\xa05\xa0times\xa0daily\xa0for\xa07\xa0to\xa010\xa0days.'
  PASSAGE_0034: 'Treatment\xa0of\xa0Initial\xa0Genital\xa0Herpes\n200\xa0mg\xa0every\xa04\xa0hours,\xa05\xa0times\xa0daily\xa0for 10\xa0days.'
  PASSAGE_0035: 'Chronic\xa0Suppressive\xa0Therapy\xa0for\xa0Recurrent\xa0Disease\n400 mg 2 times daily for up to 12 months, followed by re-evaluation. Alternative regimens have included doses ranging from 200 mg 3 times daily to 200 mg 5 times daily. The frequency and severity of episodes of untreated genital herpes may change over time. After 1 year of therapy, the frequency and severity of the patient’s genital herpes infection should be re- evaluated to assess the need for continuation of therapy with acyclovir.'
  PASSAGE_0036: 'Intermittent\xa0Therapy\n200\xa0mg\xa0every\xa04\xa0hours,\xa05\xa0times\xa0daily\xa0for\xa05\xa0days.\xa0Therapy\xa0should\xa0be initiated\xa0at\xa0the\xa0earliest\xa0sign\xa0or\xa0symptom\xa0(prodrome)\xa0of\xa0recurrence.'
  PASSAGE_0037: 'Treatment\xa0of\xa0Chickenpox\nChildren\xa0(2\xa0years\xa0of\xa0age\xa0and\xa0older)\n20 mg/kg per dose orally 4 times daily (80 mg/kg/day) for 5 days. Children over 40 kg should receive the adult dose for chickenpox.'
  PASSAGE_0038: 'Adults\xa0and\xa0Children\xa0over\xa040\xa0kg\n800\xa0mg\xa04\xa0times\xa0daily\xa0for\xa05\xa0days.\nIntravenous\xa0acyclovir\xa0is\xa0indicated\xa0for\xa0the\xa0treatment\xa0of\xa0varicella-zoster\xa0infections\xa0in immunocompromised\xa0patients.\nWhen\xa0therapy\xa0is\xa0indicated,\xa0it\xa0should\xa0be\xa0initiated\xa0at\xa0the\xa0earliest\xa0sign\xa0or\xa0symptom\xa0of\xa0chickenpox. There\xa0is\xa0no\xa0information\xa0about\xa0the\xa0efficacy\xa0of\xa0therapy\xa0initiated\xa0more\xa0than\xa024\xa0hours\xa0after\xa0onset\xa0of\xa0signs and\xa0symptoms.\nPatients\xa0With\xa0Acute\xa0or\xa0Chronic\xa0Renal\xa0Impairment\nIn\xa0patients\xa0with\xa0renal\xa0impairment,\xa0the\xa0dose of Acyclovir\xa0Capsules,\xa0Tablets,\xa0or Oral Suspension\xa0should\xa0be\xa0modified\xa0as\xa0shown\xa0in\xa0Table\xa03.\nTable\xa03.\xa0Dosage\xa0Modification\xa0for\xa0Renal\xa0Impairment'
  PASSAGE_0039: 'Hemodialysis\nFor patients who require hemodialysis, the mean plasma half-life of acyclovir during hemodialysis is approximately 5 hours. This results in a 60% decrease in plasma concentrations following a 6-hour dialysis period. Therefore, the patient’s dosing schedule should be adjusted so that an additional dose is administered after each dialysis.'
  PASSAGE_0040: 'Peritoneal\xa0Dialysis\nNo\xa0supplemental\xa0dose\xa0appears\xa0to\xa0be\xa0necessary\xa0after\xa0adjustment\xa0of\xa0the\xa0dosing interval.'
  PASSAGE_0041: 'Bioequivalence\xa0of\xa0Dosage\xa0Forms\nAcyclovir Oral Suspension\xa0was\xa0shown\xa0to\xa0be\xa0bioequivalent\xa0to Acyclovir\xa0Capsules\xa0(n\xa0=\xa020)\xa0and\xa01\xa0acyclovir\xa0800-mg\xa0tablet\xa0was\xa0shown\xa0to\xa0be\xa0bioequivalent\xa0to\xa04 acyclovir\xa0200-mg\xa0capsules\xa0(n\xa0=\xa024).'
  PASSAGE_0042: 'Acyclovir Oral Suspension, USP\xa0contains\xa0200\xa0mg of acyclovir, USP in\xa0each teaspoonful\xa0(5\xa0mL). The white to off white, banana flavored suspension is available as follows:'
  PASSAGE_0043: '1 pint bottle\xa0(473\xa0mL)\nStor\ne\xa0at\xa015°\xa0to\xa025°C\xa0(59°\xa0to\xa077°F). Protect from light.\nFor more information, go to www.novadozpharma.com or call 1-855-668-2369.\nManufactured by:\nMSN Pharmaceuticals Inc.'
  PASSAGE_0044: 'Piscataway, NJ 08854\nDistributed by:'
  PASSAGE_0045: 'Piscataway, NJ 08854-3714\nIssued on: August 2022'
